cff-version: 1.2.0
title: >-
  Fifty Years of the Portuguese Minimum Wage: Real Erosion, the Productivity
  Gap, and the Limits of Regional Identification
message: >-
  If you use this software or the accompanying analysis, please cite it using
  the metadata below. Where a version-specific record exists on Zenodo, cite
  that DOI; otherwise cite the concept DOI, which always resolves to the latest
  version.
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Diogo
    family-names: Ribeiro
    email: diogo_dj@hotmail.com
    orcid: 'https://orcid.org/0009-0001-2022-7072'
    affiliation: >-
      School of Media Arts and Design, Polytechnic of Porto, Portugal
repository-code: 'https://github.com/DiogoRibeiro7/portugal-minimum-wage-inflation'
abstract: >-
  Reproducible study of Portugal's statutory minimum wage from its introduction
  in May 1974 to 2026. The national series is parsed from the labour ministry's
  official register of acts and reconciled against both the increases that
  register states and Eurostat's independent compilation; the acts the register
  omits, and the regional schedules of Madeira and the Azores, are retrieved
  from the Diário da República by permanent identifier and read for the values
  they set. Combined with a consumer price index and a productivity series
  reaching back to the 1960s, it documents the long-run record: the real wage
  floor fell through the inflation of the late 1970s and early 1980s and did not
  regain its 1974 level for four decades, while output per worker more than
  doubled. The study also establishes why the regional variation available in
  Portugal cannot identify the price pass-through of minimum-wage policy. No
  causal claim about pass-through is made.
keywords:
  - minimum wage
  - inflation
  - price pass-through
  - labour productivity
  - Portugal
  - local projections
  - panel data
  - HICP
  - reproducible research
